<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Kyoga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Kyoga is a large, shallow lake in central Uganda, through which the Victoria <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flows on its way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a>. Much of it is fringed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> swamps and floating vegetation. It formed the northern edge of the Busoga principalities and of the lands later conquered for the British by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semei-kakungulu">Semei Kakungulu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-kyoga.jpg" alt="Lake Kyoga" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS-67_A_variety_of_lakes_are_pictured_in_Uganda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
